<br>
<b><font color=#ccof14> 2.4</font> &nbsp <font color=#008000>
Introduction to TOPCAT</font></b>: the <b>T</b>ool for <b>OP</b>erations on <b>C</b>atalogues <b>A</b>nd <b>T</b>ables,
an interactive java graphical program which has been developed by astronomers at the Virtual Astronomical Observatory.</p>
<ul>
<li> TOPCAT home: &nbsp <a href="http://www.star.bristol.ac.uk/~mbt/topcat/">http://www.star.bristol.ac.uk/~mbt/topcat/</a>
<li> TOPCAT manual: &nbsp <a href="http://www.star.bristol.ac.uk/~mbt/topcat/sun253/index.html">http://www.star.bristol.ac.uk/~mbt/topcat/sun253/index.html</a>
<li> TOPCAT tutorial: &nbsp <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20110715215030/http://andromeda.star.bris.ac.uk/topcat/tutorial/">https://web.archive.org/web/20110715215030/http://andromeda.star.bris.ac.uk/topcat/tutorial/</a></ul>
</p>

&nbsp &nbsp a. &nbsp Do you have access to the <a href="https://sites.google.com/site/alfalfaugradgroups">
UAT groups Google sites page</a>?. &nbsp <i>Hint: Find it at:
https://sites.google.com/site/alfalfaugradgroups/</i>.
If you do, go there and log in. If not, send email to Becky or David's 
gmail account. David's is jblxdwc@gmail.com.  They can add you to the 
site. When you reach the site, look the bar on the left. Under **Programs**
you will see UAT TOPCAT Docs, click there. If you have not visited this
site recently (or at all), briefly review the documents there; refer to
them as needed as we proceed.</p>

&nbsp &nbsp b. &nbsp Start up <b>TOPCAT</b> on your machine.
&nbsp <i> If you have not installed TOPCAT on your laptop or local machine, follow the 
steps in **Installing and Getting Started** for your system.  Java web start is generally
the easiest, or Mac disk images, but TOPCAT is also easy to install on Windows machines.</i></p>

&nbsp &nbsp c. &nbsp In the latter part of the **Installing and getting started** on the UAT wiki, read section on the
2.1 **Loading data from a CSV file**. What does "CSV" stands for?</p>

&nbsp &nbsp d. &nbsp Go to the public <a href="http://egg.astro.cornell.edu/alfalfa/data">
ALFALFA catalog release</a> page.  
If you have not already committed it to perfect memory, peruse the &alpha;.40 paper.
You will find detailed information on the content and format of the three
data files in the header of the ASCII versions of the data files. What does "ASCII"
stand for?</p>

&nbsp &nbsp e. &nbsp Right-click (or whatever) to download the .csv file associated with Table
1 of the &alpha;.40 paper to you computer
using the **CSV** link. Be sure you know where it is located on your disk. Then, 
using <b>TOPCAT</b>, load the .csv file into a table. How do you let <b>TOPCAT</b>
know that it is a .csv file?</p>

&nbsp &nbsp f. &nbsp Review the column headers in the Table within <b>TOPCAT</b>. They
should match the descriptions in the header of its ASCII file. At what resolution
is the <i>rms</i> calculated?</p>

&nbsp &nbsp g. &nbsp Make a plot of distance (x-axis) versus log Msun for the &alpha;.40 database. What
is log Msun?</p><br>

<!--
<b><font color=#cc0f14> Here is the Spanhauer diagram for &alpha;.40: (click for larger version)<br>
<a href="files/alph40.sdss.spanhauer.gif"><img src="files/alph40.sdss.spanhauer.gif" height=300></a><br>
</font></b></p>
-->

&nbsp &nbsp h. &nbsp Why are the points distributed so that they spread out in the vertical
   direction at nearer distances but are more confined in log Msun as the
   distance increases?</p><br>

&nbsp &nbsp g. &nbsp There appears to be a gap in the distribution near the right at
   a distance of about 220 Mpc. What causes that gap?</p><br>

&nbsp &nbsp h. &nbsp There is a less obvious gap at a distance of about 80 Mpc. What causes that gap?</p>

&nbsp &nbsp i. &nbsp There appears to be a vertical line of points at a distance of about 17 Mpc.
   What causes the apparent line?</p><br>

<!--
<b><font color=#cc0f14>
The gaps appear at frequencies corresponding to 1390 and 1350 MHz, due to RFI in the band.<br>
For 85 Mpc => cz ~ 6000 km/s => z = 0.0198 => 1420.4058/(1+z) ~1390 MHz <br>
For 230 Mpc => cz ~ 16100 km/s => z = 0.054 => 1420.4058/(1+z) ~ 1350 (won't be exact)<br>
Cause: RFI. In particular see <a href="http://www.naic.edu/~phil/rfi/rfilist.html">Phil's list</a>.
The fairly broad band at 220 Mpc is the FAA/SJU radar at 1350 MHz; note that it is not on all
the time. The other feature is probably the dome camera, which was a problem during part of
the ALFALFA data taking (until identified and fixed). The line-up at 17 Mpc is the Virgo cluster;
we assign all galaxies believed to be members to a single distance.</font></b></p>
-->

There are lots of other things you can do with <b>TOPCAT</b>. We encourage you to learn
how to exploit its capability!</p><br>
</ul>
